  "live_records": {
    "q-5192": {
      "dom_id": "step-q5192",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the center and radius of the circle whose equation is ${x}^{2}+6x+{y}^{2}=40.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+{y}^{2} & =40 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+6x)+{y}^{2} & =40 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2})+{y}^{2} & =40+{3}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2})+{y}^{2} & =49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={7}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-3))^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={7}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-3,0)$ and its radius is $7.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
