    "q-103893": {
      "dom_id": "step-q103893",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x-2y=\\frac{11}{4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x-2y & =\\frac{11}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x)+({y}^{2}-2y) & =\\frac{11}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot y+{1}^{2}) & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot y+{1}^{2}) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect square and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2} & ={2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ and its radius is $2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
